    },
    {
      "id": "kJ4s0XW9xN2ekK8UdvD6P",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -502.3933615931864,
      "y": 31942.971548939953,
      "width": 316.7998046875,
      "height": 45,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bIV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 47933879,
      "version": 20,
      "versionNonce": 2116239255,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1762617352501,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Sql Transaction:",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Sql Transaction:",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "RESLaXgxO_zdKc2KYsinv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -498.75464826538666,
      "y": 32002.99723168801,
      "width": 1084.8393688063588,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bIW",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 72004343,
      "version": 136,
      "versionNonce": 162249689,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1762617475977,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What is a Transaction in SQL?\nSimple Definition\nA Transaction is a group of SQL operations that are executed as a single unit of work. Either all\noperations succeed together, or all operations fail together (no partial execution).",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What is a Transaction in SQL?\nSimple Definition\nA Transaction is a group of SQL operations that are executed as a single unit of work. Either all operations succeed together, or all operations fail together (no partial execution).",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "xKp4CBzxX8s6icu-DYxK4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -499.0684897830746,
      "y": 32130.379541829563,
      "width": 1011.1735799793078,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bIX",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 957307289,
      "version": 116,
      "versionNonce": 2104017207,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1762617510442,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What Are SQL Transactions?\nA SQL transaction is a single, logical unit of work comprised of one or more SQL\nstatements. A transaction treats all its component statements as a single, all-or-nothing\noperation. The transaction must either fully succeed (commit), making all changes\npermanent, or entirely fail (rollback), undoing all changes as if they never happened.\n\nThe entire concept is built around the ACID properties:\n\n1.Atomicity: The \"all or nothing\" rule. A transaction must be executed entirely or not at\nall.\n\n2.Consistency: Ensures that a transaction brings the database from one valid state to\nanother, maintaining all defined rules (like constraints, triggers, etc.).\n\n3.Isolation: Guarantees that concurrent transactions do not interfere with each other. Each\ntransaction runs as if it were the only one executing.\n\n4.Durability: Once a transaction is committed, its changes are permanent, even if the\nsystem fails.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What Are SQL Transactions?\nA SQL transaction is a single, logical unit of work comprised of one or more SQL statements. A transaction treats all its component statements as a single, all-or-nothing operation. The transaction must either fully succeed (commit), making all changes permanent, or entirely fail (rollback), undoing all changes as if they never happened.\n\nThe entire concept is built around the ACID properties:\n\n1.Atomicity: The \"all or nothing\" rule. A transaction must be executed entirely or not at all.\n\n2.Consistency: Ensures that a transaction brings the database from one valid state to another, maintaining all defined rules (like constraints, triggers, etc.).\n\n3.Isolation: Guarantees that concurrent transactions do not interfere with each other. Each transaction runs as if it were the only one executing.\n\n4.Durability: Once a transaction is committed, its changes are permanent, even if the system fails.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "krh4lBwz63UQf7Rb6imMI",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -497.35401190034736,
      "y": 32657.651652914195,
      "width": 726,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bIY",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1531443065,
      "version": 44,
      "versionNonce": 1949194039,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1762617536163,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Are Transactions Only for Manipulation?\n\nNo, transactions are crucial for reading data as well. While their\nmost obvious use is for protecting Data Manipulation Language\n(DML) statements (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE), they are essential for\nimplementing Isolation during reads.\n\n1. Protecting DML Operations (The Obvious Use)\nTransactions ensure that complex changes are not half-finished.\n\nExample (Transfer Money):\n\nBEGIN TRANSACTION\n\nUPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance - 100 WHERE AccountID = 1;\n(Debit)\n\nUPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance + 100 WHERE AccountID = 2;\n(Credit)\n\nCOMMIT;\n\nIf the database crashes after step 2, the COMMIT is never reached,\nand the database automatically performs a rollback, ensuring the\nmoney isn't lost.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Are Transactions Only for Manipulation?\n\nNo, transactions are crucial for reading data as well. While their most obvious use is for protecting Data Manipulation Language (DML) statements (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE), they are essential for implementing Isolation during reads.\n\n1. Protecting DML Operations (The Obvious Use)\nTransactions ensure that complex changes are not half-finished.\n\nExample (Transfer Money):\n\nBEGIN TRANSACTION\n\nUPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance - 100 WHERE AccountID = 1; (Debit)\n\nUPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance + 100 WHERE AccountID = 2; (Credit)\n\nCOMMIT;\n\nIf the database crashes after step 2, the COMMIT is never reached, and the database automatically performs a rollback, ensuring the money isn't lost.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "V8WZaYdRCISqdoWA0JRR-",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -504.21180134788074,
      "y": 33320.93284490014,
      "width": 726,
      "height": 550,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bIa",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 862497111,
      "version": 29,
      "versionNonce": 1377707799,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1762617609583,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. Protecting Read Operations (The Critical Use)\n\nWhen you include a SELECT statement inside a transaction, you\nensure that the data you read is consistent throughout the entire\ntransaction's duration, especially in a concurrent environment.\nThis is governed by the Isolation Level.\n\nExample (Complex Report): Imagine you are generating a report that\nsums values from multiple related tables.\n\nBEGIN TRANSACTION\n\nSELECT SUM(Sales) FROM Orders;\n\nSELECT SUM(Inventory) FROM Products;\n\nCOMMIT;\n\nIf another user commits a change (e.g., they insert a new order)\nbetween steps 2 and 3, your report would be inconsistent (reading\nthe sales before the new order, but the inventory after the new\norder).",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. Protecting Read Operations (The Critical Use)\n\nWhen you include a SELECT statement inside a transaction, you ensure that the data you read is consistent throughout the entire transaction's duration, especially in a concurrent environment. This is governed by the Isolation Level.\n\nExample (Complex Report): Imagine you are generating a report that sums values from multiple related tables.\n\nBEGIN TRANSACTION\n\nSELECT SUM(Sales) FROM Orders;\n\nSELECT SUM(Inventory) FROM Products;\n\nCOMMIT;\n\nIf another user commits a change (e.g., they insert a new order) between steps 2 and 3, your report would be inconsistent (reading the sales before the new order, but the inventory after the new order).",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
